rom the missing 2x2 corner, while V-shapes shoot along the axis opposite their opening.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/409aa875/D5/1/code.py:168:    - If a landing cell lies on a sprite, recolor the entire hit sprite to maroon (9).\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/409aa875/D5/1/code.py:169:    - If a landing cell is empty background, paint that one cell according to how many projectiles arrived there: 1 projectile -> maroon (9), 2 -> blue (1), 3 -> red (2), 4 -> green (3), then continue through the visible non-background palette while skipping the background color 7.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/a32d8b75/D3/0/code.py:23:#     - Variant: the same three outer actives each receive, for example, a 2x2 L-shaped\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/a32d8b75/D6/3/code.py:41:#       - the union gives 9 visible shapes: 4 L-shapes, 4 T-shapes, 1 plus-shape\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/a32d8b75/D6/3/code.py:50:#       which is an L-shape in the lower-left of the cross skeleton\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/409aa875/D5/2/code.py:34:#         (0,1)->(1,0), (1,1)->(1,-1), etc. The visible change is the maroon trail after X.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/409aa875/D5/2/code.py:59:#       lone maroon landing dots or sprite recolors, so the user can isolate the changed branch.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/409aa875/D5/2/code.py:329:    - L-shapes use their 4-neighbor corner as the apex and fire diagonally away from the missing corner; diagonal V-shapes use their 8-neighbor apex and fire along the axis opposite the opening.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/409aa875/D5/2/code.py:337:    Key insight: Sprite orientation still determines the first rendezvous point, but a shared empty landing is no longer the final output mark; it is a right-angle redirection hub whose outgoing maroon trail reveals the new rule.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/409aa875/D9/0/code.py:27:      - original rule: yellow target becomes maroon 9\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/cod